<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=ishango-bone">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ishango bone</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ishango bone is an engraved bone tool found at Ishango, near the Semliki River in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, and believed to be about 20,000 years old. About 10 centimetres long, with a sharp piece of quartz fixed to one end, it bears 168 marks in three columns, read as tallies, a counting tool or a lunar calendar. Its dating and meaning are both debated. It was found in 1950 by the Belgian Jean de Heinzelin de Braucourt, who took it to the Royal Belgian Institute of Natural Sciences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ishango-bone.jpg" alt="Ishango bone" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joeykentin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishango_bone_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 18,000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Ishango, Congo</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Its age, with estimates from 9000 BCE to 44,000 years ago, and whether its marks are mathematical are both debated.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=ishango-bone">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
